compiler.md, “Traps of the compiled path”, line 134, a new bullet, the second, after the bullet of change 79
{+Until the switch-over, the compiler's prelude declares no =/= at RR32 and RR64, so the checker refuses r =/= 0 for an RR64 value r (ledger row 1059). Write NOT (r = 0). For an RR32 value c, the checker refuses c = 0: 'Ambiguous coercion in call to operator ='. c = d for two RR32 values compiles, and overflows the stack when it runs (ledger row 528). Bind c at RR64 first, as r: RR64 = c, and compare r.+}


Why, climb batch 36. Not a new rule: a trap that the batch changed in part. The bullet said that at RR32, = compiles and overflows the stack when it runs (row 528). Climb batch 36 made the checker refuse a call that two declarations fit equally, where neither is more specific. At c = 0, for an RR32 value c, two declarations of = fit, Equality’s and RR64’s, each through a coercion. So the checker now refuses c = 0: ‘Ambiguous coercion in call to operator =’. = of two RR32 values still compiles and overflows the stack. The bullet now says both, and keeps the way round them: bind the value at RR64 first. After the cold read, one fix. The first wording used the name c one sentence before it said what c is. It said “= of two values” without their type. And it gave no message, so a worker who met the refusal could not match it to this trap. The text now says what c is first, quotes the message, and writes c = d for two RR32 values. The cold read also asked whether this holds against the one library too. The ledger row says that the one library’s RR32 repairs it at the switch-over, so the bullet’s opening words, “Until the switch-over”, already cover it.

