revival-changes.md, “Numbers”, line 99, the fourth paragraph of the rule “A numeral converts where it stands”, its third to fifth sentences, now seven; change 184 shows a later sentence of the paragraph, and the rest did not change
A program's own number type takes a numeral through a coercion generic over its numbers, which its body declares, as value object Byte(b: ZZ32) declares coerce[\nat n, nat r, nat v\](x: NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]) where { v < 256 }[- = Byte(v.asZZ32)-]. With a radix point, write coerce[\nat n, nat m, nat r, nat v\](x: RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]). [-The coercion's body reads n, r and v as ZZ values without a bound, since a numeral's digits fit no fixed width (P-173, P-40).-]{+The static arguments of either form are naturals of any size. In the body, each converts where it stands, into the type that its place fixes. A numeral whose number does not fit there is refused: by the checker statically, by walk when the place where the program writes the numeral runs. It is a ZZ only where nothing fixes a type (P-174, P-173, P-40). So Byte's body is Byte(v).+}


Why, climb batch 36. Not a new rule: the tree now follows the rule, so the departure and the form that walk ran are cut. The paragraph said that walk, the checker and the code generator read each of a numeral’s four numbers as a ZZ, so that walk refused Byte(v), and it told a worker to write Byte(v.asZZ32) under walk, as the one library did. Climb batch 36 made each of the four numbers convert where it stands, on both paths, and took the getters out of the one library’s coercions, which now write = v (F-407). So Byte(v) runs on both paths, and the two sentences are gone, with the getter on v that the curator objected to on 22 November. The paragraph also said that a numeral whose number does not fit is refused statically. Walk now refuses it too, when the place runs, so the sentence now names both refusals. The orientation update made both changes, and its judge said to install them. The landing changed the words of the curator’s decision P-174 to match, and the decision itself is unchanged. Two departures of the compiled path are left, which the text does not name: a numeral at the type of another component, whose number does not fit, is not refused statically (row 1131); and a number at a call runs the declaration that takes it as a ZZ (row 1132). The skill writer passed both to the coordinator. After the cold read, one fix. The first wording said that walk refuses the numeral “when the place runs”. That could be the place in the coercion’s body or the place where the program writes the numeral, and a worker who tests the refusal needs to know which, to match walk’s message. The text now says “when the place where the program writes the numeral runs”, as the batch’s test shows. The cold read also asked whether the one library’s coercions still write v.asZZ32, which a worker would copy. They do not, so the cut stays.


